From 95500829e1417624c4013f58f3112f87502ee44d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wang Emma <wangzhn>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 00:46:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] fix(ui): decode task-worker stdin as UTF-8, not the OS
 locale codepage

tasks/manager.py:_encode_task_worker_payload always writes a headless task
worker's stdin as UTF-8 bytes. run_task_worker's read loop consumed it with
a plain sys.stdin.readline(), which on Windows decodes using the process
locale/codepage (e.g. GBK/cp936 on a Chinese-locale machine) instead.

Verified locally (subprocess round-trip test) that this silently corrupts
non-ASCII text rather than merely raising an error: UTF-8 bytes for a given
CJK string can decode as a *different*, valid-looking but wrong GBK string
with no exception at all. Other byte sequences are invalid GBK and produce
lone surrogates via the implicit surrogateescape handler, which later blow
up as UnicodeEncodeError whenever that text is re-serialized strictly (e.g.
json.dumps(...).encode("utf-8")) -- this is the crash a background agent
task hits whenever its spawn prompt or any tool output flowing back through
this worker contains non-ASCII content.

Fix: reconfigure this worker's stdin to UTF-8/strict before the read loop.
run_task_worker is exclusively the headless subprocess entry point (never
attached to an interactive console per its own docstring), so this cannot
regress real terminal input.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/openharness/ui/app.py | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/openharness/ui/app.py b/src/openharness/ui/app.py
index c89dc04..bdd0e70 100644
--- a/src/openharness/ui/app.py
+++ b/src/openharness/ui/app.py
@@ -135,6 +135,20 @@ async def run_task_worker(
     async def _clear_output() -> None:
         return None
 
+    # The task-manager parent always writes this worker's stdin as UTF-8
+    # bytes (see tasks/manager.py:_encode_task_worker_payload). Python's
+    # default text-mode stdin encoding follows the OS locale/codepage
+    # instead (e.g. GBK/cp936 on a Chinese-locale Windows machine), so a
+    # plain sys.stdin.readline() silently mis-decodes any non-ASCII byte,
+    # producing lone surrogates that later blow up as UnicodeEncodeError
+    # once that text is re-serialized. This worker only ever runs as a
+    # headless subprocess (see docstring above), never attached to a real
+    # interactive console, so forcing UTF-8 here is always correct.
+    try:
+        sys.stdin.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="strict")
+    except (AttributeError, ValueError):
+        pass
+
     bundle = await build_runtime(
         cwd=cwd,
         model=model,
-- 
